Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 17:28:52 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 71/71] Make the read tool serve the ask, and rebase the prompt
 on gpt-5.6

The read tool applied `offset`/`limit` only when exactly one path was passed and
dropped them silently otherwise, so a model that wanted a window inside a batch
had no way to ask for one. Every single-path call in the recorded runs carried an
`offset`: the model was not forgetting to batch, it was chasing windows through
the only door that was open. An entry is now a path or `{path, offset, limit}`,
and a hand-written normalizer accepts the shapes a model actually emits, at zero
cost on the fixed prefix.

The call budget is now derived from the model rather than hardcoded at 96_000.
The harness cuts a `function_call_output` middle-out at `truncation_policy * 1.2`,
but the binding limit is the compaction window: one call returned 93,538 bytes,
29% of an 80k window, and the runs that did that compacted twice and cost 2.4x the
run that did not. The budget is a sixth of the window, 53,333 bytes for
glm-5.3-flash, and the bound is enforced while assembling rather than while
emitting, so it holds for a batch that is entirely errors. Spending it max-min
fair rather than first-come-first-served is what makes a twenty-file batch usable.

Measured on the 44-file `wire` task, three runs: 12, 13 and 14 requests at
$0.0143-$0.0184, one compaction each, zero window-chasing calls, 44/44 citations
resolving every time. The three runs before this change spanned 12 to 33 requests
and $0.0160 to $0.0427.

Two things were tried, measured and reverted, and the reasons are in the source so
they are not tried again: dividing a top-level `limit` between the files in a
batch, which starved every file to a 40-line floor and drew 83 invitations to
continue; and leaving line numbers off unless a window was asked for, which sent
the model to `rg -n` for citations and cost 19-23 requests against 12-14.

The prompt moves from the gpt-5.5 template to gpt-5.6: 4,931 to 3,447 tokens, a
measured 1,776 off the fixed prefix of every request. All 31 lines this fork had
added to the old template carry over verbatim, including the apply_patch grammar
taken from gpt-5.2. What goes is a 1,174-token design block on colour palettes
that no other agent measured against carries, a duplicate of the skills usage
instructions that `ext/skills` already injects when skills exist, and two rules
that contradicted our own: a 60-second wait ceiling against the `yield_time_ms`
guidance, and "read the codebase first" against reading a window rather than a
whole file.

